// EXPECT_RUNTIME_PANIC unhandled Fail
//
// Registry 221.1 #680, the reported form: a throw from a `fail(e) -> never`
// arm was swallowed and the program carried on with exit 0.
//
// Measured before the fix, verbatim: `DB: about to throw / HANDLER: entered /
// AFTER`, EXIT=0. The arm is declared `-> never`, so by the type there is no
// way back out of it -- and the observable behaviour was exactly "it returned
// and all is well".
//
// The mechanism is a double dispatch, not a mis-aimed landing pad. The
// dispatcher swaps `_nova_handler_Fail` to `current->prev` before invoking the
// arm, so the arm's own throw finds NO handler and unwinds to the nearest
// fail-frame -- which is the frame this very `with` pushed, and whose
// else-branch means "handled". One frame doing two jobs: landing pad for the
// body's throws, and "the error was dealt with". The second job ate the arm's
// throw.
//
// Fixed by shielding: the frame is marked while its own arm runs, and
// `nova_fail_landing()` skips shielded frames when choosing where a throw
// lands. The frame stays in the chain, because the arm READS its payload out
// of it -- an earlier attempt that unlinked the frame instead broke five
// conformance tests for exactly that reason.
//
// Kill-switch NOVA_KILL_ARM_SHIELD=1 restores the pre-fix behaviour on the
// same binary: `AFTER`, exit 0.
module standalone.p680_arm_throw_escapes_typed

type P680Err { code int }

fn db() Fail[P680Err] Io -> () {
    println("DB: about to throw")
    throw P680Err { code: 1 }
}

fn main() Io -> () {
    with Fail = effect Fail { fail(_e) -> never { println("HANDLER: entered"); throw P680Err { code: 2 } } } {
        db()
    }
    println("AFTER")
}
